  "grammar": {
    "title": "Object pronouns: me, him, her, us",
    "points": [
      "After a verb or a preposition, use the object pronouns: mij/me (me), jou/je (you), u (you, polite), hem (him), haar (her), ons (us), jullie (you, plural), hen/ze (them).",
      "me and je are the short, unstressed forms and the normal choice in speech: Kun je me helpen? Use mij and jou for emphasis: Hij helpt mij, niet jou.",
      "hen is used for people, mostly in writing. In speech ze is much more common: Ik zie ze morgen.",
      "Things: a de-word becomes hem, a het-word becomes het. De fiets? Ik zoek hem. Het boek? Ik lees het.",
      "u, ons and jullie look the same as subject and object."
    ],
    "examples": [
      { "nl": "Kunt u mij helpen?", "en": "Can you help me?" },
      { "nl": "Ik bel je morgen op.", "en": "I'll call you tomorrow." },
      { "nl": "De dokter helpt hem.", "en": "The doctor helps him." },
      { "nl": "Ik zie haar vandaag.", "en": "I'm seeing her today." },
      { "nl": "Tom gaat met ons mee.", "en": "Tom is coming with us." },
      { "nl": "Mijn telefoon? Ik zoek hem.", "en": "My phone? I'm looking for it." }
    ],
    "exercises": [
